I fixed it in `packages/compiler-sfc/src/style/pluginScoped.ts`: a scoped `@media` query nested inside another rule now gets the scope attribute, so it no longer loses to the original `background-color`.

**Cause:** when a scoped rule contains nested rules, Vue moves that rule's own declarations into a `&[data-v-xxx] { ... }` block. That block carries the scope attribute, which makes it more specific. Declarations sitting directly inside a nested at-rule like `@media` or `@container` were left without that wrapper, so they were less specific and lost.

**Fix:** I pulled the wrapping logic into a new helper, `extractAndWrapNodes`. It now runs on the rule itself and on each at-rule nested directly inside it. Comments are also moved along with the declarations so they stay next to them.

I checked it with the issue's case in a throwaway test (since deleted). `.foo { background-color: red; .bar {} @media (...) { background-color: green; .baz { color: red } } }` now compiles to:

```css
.foo {
&[data-v-test] { background-color: red }
.bar[data-v-test] {}
@media only screen and (max-width: 800px) {
&[data-v-test] { background-color: green }
.baz[data-v-test] { color: red }
}
}
```

The existing `compileStyle.spec.ts` suite still passes (25 tests). I didn't add a permanent test for this case.

One limit: only at-rules one level inside the rule get this treatment. A `@media` nested inside another `@media` would still have the original problem.

